[#:seon.task{:name "a-jvm-run-answers-evaluated-but-not-recorded"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Two MCP JVM-mode evaluations (lane health-noise, 2026-10-06) answered 'evaluated but not recorded' (`:seon.batch/applied` true, :tx-count 0) after a 2.6 s form returning a large map: a run answered without its one synchronous settlement, which CLAUDE.md requires to be durable before the run answers. Reproduce with a JVM-mode form that runs ~2-3 s and returns a large map; find which stage drops the settlement (size bound? timeout?) and fix at that producer so the run settles or answers a declared error. Done when the repro settles (the evaluation is stored) or returns the named error alternative, with a regression."
             :must-read #{seon.batch/evaluate-batch seon.turn.batch/settle}}
 #:seon.task{:name
            "an-earlier-redefinition-of-a-host-function-fails-as-a-core-fault",
            :kind :function,
            :intent
            "produced-failures records a form's failure as a core fault when its producer is a compiled host Var this batch has not redefined. An agent that redefined a host function (e.g. `my.fs/read`) in an EARLIER batch and whose interpreted version then fails is still recorded as a core fault, and root is notified, although the failing code is the agent's. Correct: compare the definition digest that ran (interpreted on the branch) with the compiled Var's; a branch-only version is the agent's own mistake, delivered only. Proof: defn a host function's replacement in one evaluation, call it failing in the next, assert no signature is recorded.",
            :must-read #{seon.batch/produced-failures}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-deftest-fixture-observation-is-dropped-by-the-runtime-batch"
  :kind :function
  :intent "`seon.fn-test/fixture-observations-survive-static-admission-and-the-fold` (fn_test.clj:1656) is red on HEAD and on default: the runtime batch entity for a deftest carrying ^{`:seon.test/fixture-observation` ...} has no `:seon.test/fixture-observation` (line 1671 asserts it). Found while fixing the check JVM's dependency analysis (410 pass / 2 fail / 8 error, then 494 / 1 / 0). Correct: the metadata reaches the stored test entity. Done when that deftest is green."
  :must-read #{seon.batch/evaluate-batch}
  :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/check-analysis/analysis-notes.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-request-cost-counts-only-the-evaluation-arm"
             :kind :function
             :intent "`seon.batch/arm-measures` sums the allocation of the evaluation arms only, so `:seon.cost/alloc-mb` of a request that defines one function and one test reads 22 MB while the JVM allocated 1.0 GB (default f54e73c83, 2026-10-09: 709 MB on virtual threads in the test step, 296 MB on pool threads, 37 MB on the writer thread; JFR in the evidence). The agent cannot see the cost its request caused. Done when `seon.batch/request-cost` carries the allocation of the stages that ran for the request (test step and settlement included) or names the stages it excludes as data, and the 1.0 GB request reports within 2x of it."
             :must-read #{seon.batch/arm-measures seon.batch/request-cost seon.test/run}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-09/slow-alloc.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "stamped-ns-has-no-reader"
             :kind :function
             :intent "`:seon.batch/stamped-ns` (an AtomicLong each world carries) was read only by the transaction stamps' milliseconds, which `seon.profile/transaction-observation` no longer produces (FEEDBACK-LOOP stage 5). It is still made by `seon.turn.world/agent-world`, `seon.turn.world/jvm-world` and the batch world in `seon.turn/evaluate-sources`, and declared in `resources/seon/schemas/seon.batch.edn`. Done when no world carries it and its schema and its predicate `seon.batch/stamped-clock?` are deleted."
             :must-read #{seon.turn.world/agent-world seon.turn.world/jvm-world seon.turn/evaluate-sources seon.batch/stamped-clock?}}

 #:seon.task{:name "an-unchanged-interpreted-definition-shows-no-cost-entry"
             :kind :function
             :intent "`seon.batch/cost-diff` flags every sample of an interpreted scope (`:seon.profile/context`) as :interpreted and emits it, so a definition the agent did not change comes back as an entry whenever it ran interpreted, against the \"unchanged is unchanged\" and \"like for like only\" rulings (plan §7). Reproduced on default 6aca2052 as agent chain5-findings (2026-10-10): `(in-ns 'my.program) (search (seon.db/db) \"match-row\")` after defining nothing but one scratch var came back with `:seon.cost/diff` entries for `my.program/search` (:self-us, #{:interpreted :new}) and `my.program/match-row` (#{:interpreted}, and #{:interpreted :new} cold), although main stores `[my.program/search :seon.profile/context]` under the very same cold digest 09096b76..., and root's prepare-merge! and accept-merge! replies (chain run 5, gate run 74e4621e2005) listed `my.program/search` and five `seon.test-support` helpers (held-handle, with-database, run-database-body, with-branched-database, execution-handle) though only `my.program/match-row` changed. Cause at the producer: `cost-diff` decides `:changed` only by comparing a sample digest with main's SAME-scope sample digest, and an interpreted scope has no main value (main's functions are compiled), so a missing main sample reads as :new and :interpreted alone admits the entry. Correct: a live sample is a change only when its definition digest differs from the digest main holds for that symbol (the stored sample of either scope, else main's program row `:seon.program/definition-digest`, read once by the callers `seon.mcp/with-diff` and `seon.context/branch-cost` beside `seon.db/stored-costs`); a symbol main has no definition for is :new. Proof: `seon.request-cost-test/an-interpreted-sample-of-the-digest-main-holds-shows-no-entry` (two rows). The row in `seon.request-cost-test/after-a-restart-the-first-call-compares-against-the-stored-cold-value` expects an unchanged interpreted sample (same digest) as an entry and changes with this fix: root's interpreted definition on main whose digest equals main's is no entry either."
             :must-read #{seon.batch/cost-diff seon.mcp/with-diff seon.context/branch-cost seon.db/stored-costs seon.db/sink-digests}}
 #:seon.task{:name "an-entrances-self-time-is-its-forms-work"
             :kind :function
             :intent "An entrance whose self time is the evaluated form's own work must never be given a ratio. Root's JVM-mode reply that ran `seon.competency/start-run!` (chain run 5, 2026-10-10) carried `seon.turn.batch/jvm-evaluate` :cold-self-us 106063.0 against main's 25739.0, ratio 4.12, #{:changed}; the self time of `seon.turn.batch/jvm-evaluate` is whatever form it evaluates (two different forms are not like for like), so the ratio is a false regression (plan §7: only reliable, like-for-like data is surfaced). Main now stores 106063.0 for it, so the next JVM form compares with that form's work. Rule to name in the owner: a function declared an entrance (its job is to run a caller's form: `seon.turn.batch/jvm-evaluate` and the SCI evaluation entrances) is declared once as data at its definition (`:seon.profile/entrance? true` in its schema attribute map, read where `seon.profile/cell` is made), `seon.batch/cost-diff` gives its samples no :ratio and the explicit `:seon.cost.comparison/unknown :entrance`, and the entrance's self time is not what a reply's cost shows as the form's cost (that is the reply's own `:seon.cost/entrance`). Decide in the owner whether the entrance sample is stored at all."
             :must-read #{seon.batch/cost-diff seon.profile/cell seon.profile/transaction-observation seon.turn.batch/jvm-evaluate}
             :evidence #{"docs/seon/plan.md"}}
]
